## Approach

Use the **Composite Graph API** (`POST /services/data/vXX.X/composite/graph`), not the plain Composite API. It maps exactly onto the requirements:

- **Each graph is one unit of work** — atomic (all subrequests commit or all roll back) and **independent** of every other graph. A failure in `SO-1001` rolls back only that graph; `SO-1002` is still saved.
- **`graphId`** is the order number, so results are identified per order.
- Inside a graph, the Opportunity references the Account upsert result via `@{acct_1001.id}`, so the lookup is resolved at commit time.
- The Account upsert uses the standard external-ID upsert (`PATCH /sobjects/Account/Customer_Number__c/<value>`), which is supported as a graph subrequest.

Notes:
- `Customer_Number__c` is included in the body for readability; it is redundant with the URL but harmless (and keeps the value set if the field were ever non-unique).
- `Amount` is sent as a JSON number; `CloseDate` as `YYYY-MM-DD`.
- No `allOrNone` flag exists here — atomicity is per graph by definition.

```http
POST /services/data/v62.0/composite/graph HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "graphs": [
    {
      "graphId": "SO-1001",
      "compositeRequest": [
        {
          "method": "PATCH",
          "url": "/services/data/v62.0/sobjects/Account/Customer_Number__c/C-1001",
          "referenceId": "acct_1001",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Alder Creek Cafe",
            "Customer_Number__c": "C-1001"
          }
        },
        {
          "method": "POST",
          "url": "/services/data/v62.0/sobjects/Opportunity",
